itive solution to\n\\[\\frac 1{x^2-10x-29}+\\frac1{x^2-10x-45}-\\frac 2{x^2-10x-69}=0.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"8415990e98f9feccdbf6d04d680a31d7bb742ed8298c94d849d3cd2fed7ae8a7"},"seconds":63.41894173622131,"seed":1068363319391886497,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the equation\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{x^2-10x-29} + \\frac{1}{x^2-10x-45} - \\frac{2}{x^2-10x-69} = 0,\n\\]\nwe start by introducing a substitution to simplify the expression. Let \\( y = x^2 - 10x \\). Then the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{y-29} + \\frac{1}{y-45} - \\frac{2}{y-69} = 0.\n\\]\n\nNext, we find a common denominator for the fractions. The common denominator is \\((y-29)(y-45)(y-69)\\). Rewriting each fraction with this common denominator, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{(y-45)(y-69) + (y-29)(y-69) - 2(y-29)(y-45)}{(y-29)(y-45)(y-69)} = 0.\n\\]\n\nSince the denominator is non-zero, we can focus on the numerator:\n\\[\n(y-45)(y-69) + (y-29)(y-69) - 2(y-29)(y-45) = 0.\n\\]\n\nWe expand each term:\n\\[\n(y-45)(y-69) = y^2 - 114y + 3105,\n\\]\n\\[\n(y-29)(y-69) = y^2 - 98y + 2001,\n\\]\n\\[\n2(y-29)(y-45) = 2(y^2 - 74y + 1305) = 2y^2 - 148y + 2610.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these back into the equation, we have:\n\\[\n(y^2 - 114y + 3105) + (y^2 - 98y + 2001) - (2y^2 - 148y + 2610) = 0.\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\\[\ny^2 - 114y + 3105 + y^2 - 98y + 2001 - 2y^2 + 148y - 2610 = 0.\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression:\n\\[\n(y^2 + y^2 - 2y^2) + (-114y - 98y + 148y) + (3105 + 2001 - 2610) = 0,\n\\]\n\\[\n0y^2 - 64y + 2496 = 0,\n\\]\n\\[\n-64y + 2496 = 0.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( y \\):\n\\[\n-64y = -2496,\n\\]\n\\[\ny = \\frac{2496}{64},\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 39.\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\( y = x^2 - 10x \\). Thus, we have:\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x = 39.\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation:\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x - 39 = 0.\n\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -10 \\), and \\( c = -39 \\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-39)}}{2 \\cdot 1},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{100 + 156}}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{256}}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm 16}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 + 16}{2} = 13,\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 - 16}{2} = -3.\n\\]\n\nSince we are asked for the positive solution, we have:\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{13}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
